WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.462
Operating DNSSEC is mostly key
management, automated and audited.

2
00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:09.810
NIST recommends a hardware security module
for important keys, usually the KSK,

3
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.970
signature validity of
about five to seven days,

4
00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.563
and key lifetimes of one to three years.

5
00:00:15.563 --> 00:00:23.867
Its acceptable algorithms include RSA with
SHA-256 at 2,048 bits or more and ECDSA

6
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:31.800
P-256 and P-384, and it prefers ECDSA,
because smaller keys keep responses small.

7
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.204
On denial of existence, Windows
labels NSEC3 recommended,

8
00:00:37.204 --> 00:00:44.522
while NIST notes NSEC3 relies on SHA-1
and calls NSEC the more prudent approach;

9
00:00:44.522 --> 00:00:50.038
compact denial of existence, RFC
9824, is what Azure DNS uses.

10
00:00:50.038 --> 00:00:55.363
Signing grows responses, so EDNS zero
and fragmentation handling matter.

11
00:00:55.363 --> 00:00:57.959
And a zone signed in
one view but not another

12
00:00:57.959 --> 00:01:00.700
fails as an application
or certificate error.

13
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.719
Moving a signed zone has its own
sequence, run during Chapter 10's

14
00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:08.737
Phase 3 so the new chain validates
before the decommission gate.

15
00:01:08.737 --> 00:01:13.237
First, finish any rollover in progress
and start none during the move.

16
00:01:13.237 --> 00:01:16.871
Second, keep the old signing
until the new chain validates:

17
00:01:16.871 --> 00:01:20.353
sign on the new platform,
publish the new DS at the parent,

18
00:01:20.353 --> 00:01:25.350
confirm from an independent validator,
and only then stop signing at the source.

19
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:30.337
Third, watch the validators, because
clients see only generic failures.

20
00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:34.909
Fourth, an AD-signed internal zone
landing on Azure Private DNS,

21
00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:38.250
which can't sign, is a
documented risk decision.

22
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.969
The book corrects two compensations
the source guide listed:

23
00:01:41.969 --> 00:01:45.469
Azure virtual network traffic
isn't encrypted by default,

24
00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:49.188
and Private Link doesn't make
in-network answers tamper-proof.

25
00:01:49.188 --> 00:01:54.036
Removing DNSSEC is the reverse:
Microsoft documents that the DS record

26
00:01:54.036 --> 00:01:58.638
comes off the parent and its TTL
expires before the zone is unsigned.

27
00:01:58.638 --> 00:02:03.550
Fifth, remove retired trust anchors
after the ninety-day validation period.
